00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:43.340
for the rest of this course.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.410
So in this lesson, I'm just
gonna go over the basics

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:48.880
about how ggplot works
on a conceptual level.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.450
We're not gonna do any coding yet,

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.240
though I will show you the anatomy

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.560
of how the code structure
and syntax works for ggplot.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.260
All right, so let's get into it.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:01.550
So the gg in ggplot stands
for the grammar of graphics.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:02.980
The grammar of graphics provides

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:06.400
a more intuitive way to
create and customize figures.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.960
Just like a sentence is based

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:11.150
around the combination of
nouns, verbs, and adjectives

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:12.640
and other elements that work together

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.950
to contribute different facets
to the meaning of a sentence,

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.690
data visualizations are made

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:20.210
of several key elements
that work together.

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:21.670
So the most basic structure

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:25.510
of a data visualization begins
with the data themselves.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:27.550
Then various columns or variables

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:28.970
of that data set are mapped

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:32.080
onto different aesthetic
properties of the figure.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.350
So for example, certain
variables are mapped

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:38.220
to the X and the Y axis and
then you have other variables,

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:39.680
in this case, variable three,

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.560
which is mapped to identify
the point color in the figure.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.230
Anytime a variable is used
to describe an aesthetic

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.720
or visual component of
the figure in this way,

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.700
that is called mapping a variable

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.130
to the aesthetic properties of the plot.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:56.010
And finally, those aesthetic
mappings need to be presented

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.880
using a geometric object.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.850
So this is what determines
whether a figure will be

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.430
a scatter plot, a box plot, line plot,

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:06.600
or whatever other type of
plot you want to create.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.040
So far, the three elements we've
gone over include the data,

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:13.240
the aesthetic mapping, and
then the geometry of the plot.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:16.050
But you have many other
elements that might be

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.320
the coordinates of a plot, or what part

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:19.720
of the data you're gonna be showing

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.990
as well as other visual
properties of the figure

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.710
that don't particularly
have to do with the data,

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:26.940
which in this case is called the theme,

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:28.800
which might affect the font size,

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.470
the font color, et cetera of the plot.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:33.050
So now let's just do a quick overview

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.540
of the anatomy of using ggplot

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:39.530
or the syntax of how ggplot
functions come together.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.910
So first you begin with
the ggplot function.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:45.490
Now note that the package
is called ggplot2,

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:47.110
but the basic function you begin with

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:49.090
is just ggplot without the two.

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.453
Then the first argument
to the ggplot function

00:02:51.453 --> 00:02:54.370
are the data that you're
going to be using.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:55.830
And note that I'm trying to maintain

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:58.780
the same color scheme here
between these different elements

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.720
and the actual code and
syntax here that I'm showing.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.720
The next element of the ggplot
function is the aesthetics.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.480
And these are the general aesthetics

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.790
that apply to the whole plot.

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.760
So for example, the most common aesthetics

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.450
are going to be adding
in using the aes function

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:20.240
are the X and the Y values.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:22.020
So what aspects of the data map

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:25.650
to the X and to the Y
coordinates of this plot?

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.610
Then, this is a little different

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:29.500
than anything that
you've been doing so far

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.470
if you're familiar with the basics of R.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.730
But then to add functions to
this, to add elements to this,

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:38.083
we're gonna just use this plus sign here,

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.120
add a new row, and now we're going

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.340
to add the geometry to the plot.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.530
And this is where you can create a plot

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:47.630
that's just a scatter plot, a box plot,

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:49.700
or whatever other type
of plot you're creating.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:51.320
But for the sake of this example

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:55.040
we're just gonna create a
scatter plot and that is done

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.880
by using the geom point, geom
underscore point function.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:01.990
And within that geometry,

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:04.500
we can add another aesthetic element.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:08.130
So again, using the aes
function, and now we can map

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.820
out different values
from the data to things

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:14.710
like the color of the points
or the fill of the points

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:16.530
or any other aesthetic property

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:18.167
that might be connected to the data.

00:04:18.167 --> 00:04:20.150
And remember that because these points,

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:22.400
these different elements are connected

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:24.790
to different aspects of the data,

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:26.770
we literally, we make that connection,

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:30.260
we make that mapping by just saying equals

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:32.450
and then whatever the name of the variable

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:35.240
or the column in that data
that you want to refer to.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:38.650
So in the original example,
we had variable one goes

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:41.350
to the x-axis, variable
two goes to the Y axis,

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:44.290
and then within geom
point, variable three goes

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:45.800
to the color of the points.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:47.790
And finally, we can add another plus

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.930
and add whatever other
element we would like to add.

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:52.879
And there's many, many
different elements to look at.

00:04:52.879 --> 00:04:55.230
Don't worry if this is a bit confusing

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.420
or if this seems like a lot to remember.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.940
There's actually a really good cheat sheet

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:00.080
that ggplot developed.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:01.770
There's a link that I put down below

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:03.230
to access this cheat sheet.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:05.310
I actually refer to it all the time.

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:06.400
Here's my version.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:09.450
It's kind of old, but there
might be an updated version.

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:12.010
So that is basically the anatomy

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:14.940
of how a ggplot function works.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.180
All right, well, thank
